1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,304
Case studies, part one: liver cancer.

2
00:00:03,304 --> 00:00:09,053
We use RMTI as a triage layer for hepatocellular
carcinoma surveillance.

3
00:00:09,053 --> 00:00:14,003
The question is: which patient groups need surveillance
first,

4
00:00:14,003 --> 00:00:17,357
and which lever moves each group the most?

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,563
A reminder before we start.

6
00:00:20,563 --> 00:00:25,850
RMTI is a research prototype: not validated, not a
medical device,

7
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:29,695
and not for patient-care or emergency decisions.

8
00:00:29,695 --> 00:00:35,943
Everything here uses illustrative values, published
rows, or aggregate counts.

9
00:00:35,943 --> 00:00:37,865
No patient data appears.

10
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:42,050
Here is how the inputs map to the liver.

11
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:44,885
L comes from a validated risk score.

12
00:00:44,885 --> 00:00:50,475
E is the share of the at-risk population in the
surveillance footprint.

13
00:00:50,475 --> 00:00:53,625
V is liver reserve, from the ALBI grade.

14
00:00:53,625 --> 00:01:01,027
And rho is surveillance effectiveness, on a published
ladder from none, at 0.15, to optimised,

15
00:01:01,027 --> 00:01:01,657
at 0.65.

16
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:10,521
Four published cohort rows from the RMTI HCC calculator:
untreated hepatitis B, active viral cirrhosis,

17
00:01:10,521 --> 00:01:15,001
alcohol-related cirrhosis, and cured hepatitis C
cirrhosis.

18
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:22,594
The after values for resilience are back-solved to match
the published table, so they are scenarios,

19
00:01:22,594 --> 00:01:23,885
not observations.

20
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:30,084
Each group travels the cube: likelihood, exposure,
vulnerability.

21
00:01:30,084 --> 00:01:34,824
Notice how far out untreated hepatitis B sits on
likelihood.

22
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:43,820
With today's surveillance, untreated hepatitis B is the
only group in tier 3, high, with R of 0.31.

23
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:47,926
The three cirrhosis groups sit in tier 2, moderate.

24
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,689
After the improvement package, cured hepatitis C falls
to tier 1, low.

25
00:01:54,689 --> 00:02:01,760
But untreated hepatitis B stays in tier 3, high, even
though its R falls by 39 percent.

26
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:04,954
Lever one: resilience.

27
00:02:04,954 --> 00:02:10,243
Walk untreated hepatitis B up the surveillance ladder.

28
00:02:10,243 --> 00:02:17,000
None, usual care, outreach, specialist: R falls, but
stays in tier 3.

29
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:27,283
Only optimised surveillance, ultrasound plus AFP with
reliable access to cure, at 0.65, brings R to 0.12,

30
00:02:27,283 --> 00:02:29,633
below 0.15, into tier 2.

31
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:35,101
Which single lever would be enough to leave tier 3?

32
00:02:35,101 --> 00:02:41,486
Resilience needs to reach at least 0.57, which the
optimised rung exceeds.

33
00:02:41,486 --> 00:02:48,302
Vulnerability would have to fall to 0.26, below the
lowest ALBI anchor of 0.35,

34
00:02:48,302 --> 00:02:50,891
so reserve alone cannot do it.

35
00:02:50,891 --> 00:02:55,982
Exposure would need to fall to 0.34, or likelihood to
0.43.

36
00:02:57,050 --> 00:02:59,653
Lever two: treat the cause.

37
00:02:59,653 --> 00:03:06,883
In the published table, untreated hepatitis B carries a
likelihood of 0.90,

38
00:03:06,883 --> 00:03:09,968
and suppressed hepatitis B 0.20.

39
00:03:09,968 --> 00:03:19,704
Move only that lever, holding the other inputs of the
untreated group, and R falls from 0.31 to 0.07,

40
00:03:19,704 --> 00:03:21,825
from tier 3 to tier 2.

41
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:25,382
Lever three: liver reserve.

42
00:03:25,382 --> 00:03:32,553
In the framework, fibrosis progression and
decompensation raise vulnerability.

43
00:03:32,553 --> 00:03:41,562
Using the ALBI anchors as a what-if on the active viral
cirrhosis group: at grade one, R is 0.067;

44
00:03:41,562 --> 00:03:47,537
at grade two, 0.115; at grade three, 0.164, crossing
into tier 3.

45
00:03:47,537 --> 00:03:51,398
This is a what-if sweep, not patient data.

46
00:03:52,450 --> 00:03:58,181
To ground V in real measurements, we use the Faria et
al. cohort from Harvard Dataverse.

47
00:03:58,181 --> 00:04:04,759
From measured albumin and bilirubin, each person has an
ALBI grade, and we use only the group counts,

48
00:04:04,759 --> 00:04:06,582
never patient-level records.

49
00:04:07,650 --> 00:04:12,701
Weighting the grade anchors by those counts gives
vulnerability:

50
00:04:12,701 --> 00:04:16,647
0.52 for the cancer cohort and 0.48 for cirrhosis.

51
00:04:16,647 --> 00:04:19,646
A real measurement, feeding one lever.

52
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:27,733
Putting the levers side by side for untreated hepatitis
B, each moved by ten points,

53
00:04:27,733 --> 00:04:34,180
from largest to smallest: vulnerability, exposure,
likelihood and resilience.

54
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:36,273
R falls by 0.057 at most.

55
00:04:36,273 --> 00:04:42,134
The ranking depends on the group, which is why RMTI is
run per cohort.

56
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:45,535
What this shows and does not show.

57
00:04:45,535 --> 00:04:50,480
The arithmetic and the levers are RMTI's own, applied to
published rows.

58
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:56,454
But fibrosis-stage inputs are not in the current
calculators, so ALBI anchors stand in,

59
00:04:56,454 --> 00:05:02,360
the after values are scenarios, and nothing here is
validated against incident cancer.

60
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:06,412
Surveillance decisions rest with clinicians and
guidelines.

61
00:05:07,450 --> 00:05:12,622
That is RMTI on the liver: one equation, three levers
you can pull,

62
00:05:12,622 --> 00:05:15,787
and a clear view of what each would take.

63
00:05:15,787 --> 00:05:17,485
I'm Dr. Cesar Marolla.

64
00:05:17,485 --> 00:05:22,503
The code is open under Apache 2.0, the demo uses no
patient data,

65
00:05:22,503 --> 00:05:25,205
and nothing here is medical advice.
